# AI-Assisted Software Project Collaboration Workflow

## PURPOSE
Controlled zero-cost collaboration between:
- Human Project Owner (vision, decisions, testing, Git)
- Architect AI (ChatGPT / Gemini / Grok) — task design + plan review
- Developer Agent (Opencode or equivalent) — plan creation + code execution

Goals:
- Correct software
- Architecture protection
- Prevent scope creep
- Clean project memory
- Minimal context pollution for free-tier models

## ROLES

### 1. Project Owner (Human)
- Defines goals and scope
- Makes final decisions
- Runs all tests
- Handles all Git operations
- Reviews final results

### 2. Architect AI
- Understands architecture
- Creates task.md
- Reviews plan.md
- Writes fix.md when needed
- Prevents complexity and scope expansion
- Does not write production code unless explicitly ordered

### 3. Developer Agent
- Reads all project documents
- Inspects code
- Creates and revises plan.md
- Implements only approved plans
- Never makes major architecture decisions
- Never touches Git

## PROJECT DOCUMENTS

### Permanent
- `AGENTS.md` — AI working rules (rare changes)
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` — current design, modules, dependencies (update only when architecture actually changes)
- `docs/LOG.md` — append-only history

### Temporary (per task)
- `docs/task.md` — immutable problem definition (locked after creation)
- `docs/plan.md` — living implementation plan (overwritten every round)
- `docs/fix.md` — latest correction note from Architect (created from Round 2; overwritten each failed review)

## DEVELOPMENT CYCLE

### Phase 1: Planning (Round 1)
1. Architect creates `docs/task.md` containing:
   - Problem
   - Goal
   - Constraints
   - Target files / areas
   - Success criteria
2. `task.md` is now locked and immutable.
3. Developer Agent reads `task.md` + architecture + code, then creates `docs/plan.md`.
4. No code changes allowed in this phase.

### Phase 2: Review & Revision Loop
Architect reviews `plan.md` against `task.md`.

Review criteria (must all pass):
- Fits existing architecture
- Correct modules selected
- Scope is minimal and bounded
- No unrelated work
- Low risk of breaking existing features
- Does not increase future maintenance cost

Outcomes:
- APPROVED → proceed to Phase 3
- NEEDS REVISION:
  - Architect writes/overwrites `docs/fix.md` with precise failure reasons
  - Developer Agent reads `task.md` + latest `fix.md` and overwrites `plan.md`
  - Return to Architect for re-review

3-Strikes Reset Rule:
If still failing at Round 4 or Round 5 → stop. Architect rewrites `task.md` from scratch and restart cycle. Current task is considered too ambiguous.

### Phase 3: Implementation
Developer Agent executes the approved `plan.md` exactly.
- Changes must stay strictly within task bounds
- No extra features, refactors, or “improvements” outside the plan

### Phase 4: Completion
1. Human runs tests and handles Git.
2. If design changed → update `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.
3. Append summary to `docs/LOG.md`.
4. Delete temporary files: `task.md`, `plan.md`, `fix.md`.

## HARD RULES

Rule 1 — Architecture Protection  
Before any addition ask: “Does this belong in the current architecture?”  
Prefer existing mechanisms over new code.

Rule 2 — Small Changes Only  
Small task + small plan + small code change.  
Large rewrites are forbidden without explicit new task.

Rule 3 — No Scope Expansion  
Task boundaries are absolute.  
Unrelated work requires a new task.

Rule 4 — Context Hygiene  
Keep every temporary file short and focused.  
No conversation history dumping into files.  
Clear temporary files at end of every task.

Rule 5 — Developer Agent Constraints  
- Never touch Git
- Never invent architecture decisions
- Never expand beyond approved plan
- Always prefer reading existing code and documents before proposing changes
